<ArticleLayout slug="infant-flour-production-by-extrusion-cooking">
  <p>
    Precooked flours are today a great success with consumers compared with
    native flours, because of their convenience, their improved nutritional
    and organoleptic qualities, and their extended shelf life.
  </p>
  <p>
    Extrusion cooking stands apart from other processes through several
    competitive advantages: versatility; ease of integration into complete
    production lines; wide scope for adjustment and automation; high energy
    efficiency; high production capacity; and the specific nutritional and
    physico-chemical characteristics it gives to the flours thus processed.
  </p>

  <h2>Production line and process control</h2>
  <p>
    The manufacture of extruded flours goes through the stages described in
    the following diagram:
  </p>
  <!-- TODO: contenu client — cette figure porte du texte français
       incrusté dans l'image : faire produire une version anglaise.
       L'alt et la légende sont traduits, pas le visuel. -->
  <figure>
    <Image
      src={figLigne}
      alt="Diagram of the stages of an extruded flour production line"
      widths={[600, 900]}
      width={900}
      quality={70}
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 700px"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>The stages in the manufacture of extruded flours.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    As regards the equipment and its operation, the following points will need
    particular attention:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      control of hygienic quality (HACCP approach): limiting the risk of water
      pick-up after extrusion,
    </li>
    <li>control of the degree of cooking,</li>
    <li>
      control of the correct formulation of the flour: effective dosing and
      mixing systems, in particular for the mineral and vitamin premix to be
      incorporated and its even distribution throughout the flour,
    </li>
    <li>
      control of the final particle size, to limit the presence of hard spots
      when the porridges are reconstituted; a sieving system with recycling of
      rejects.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Applications of extruded flours in human food</h2>
  <p>
    Extruded flours are mainly used as a complementary food, in infant
    nutrition or in humanitarian intervention programmes. In both cases, they
    are obtained by formulating blends of flours and by systematic mineral and
    vitamin fortification. The aim is to balance as well as possible the
    qualitative and quantitative intake of macro- and micro-constituents, in
    order to come as close as possible to nutritional recommendations
    regarding:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the energy value provided by the fats,</li>
    <li>
      the nutritional quality of the proteins incorporated: the amino acid
      composition can be adjusted through a good cereal/pulse
      complementarity,
    </li>
    <li>essential micronutrients (minerals, vitamins, ω3 fatty acids),</li>
    <li>
      limiting the factors liable to cause intolerance or discomfort, such as
      gluten and fibre for the young child.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <figure>
    <Image
      src={bouillieImg}
      alt="Reconstituted infant porridge"
      widths={[600, 900]}
      width={900}
      quality={70}
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 700px"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </figure>

  <h2>Infant flours produced by extrusion cooking</h2>
  <p>
    As regards infant flours, the WHO recommends using them only from
    6&nbsp;months. At that age, infant flours can be introduced as a
    complement and then as a gradual substitute for breastfeeding, after
    rehydration with water or milk. The rehydration rate depends on the age of
    the child, so as to suit its physiological needs: a slight thickening of
    the infant milk given in a bottle (follow-on formula), then increasingly
    thick porridges given with a spoon (children over 12&nbsp;months).
  </p>
  <p>
    At this level, extrusion cooking is of specific interest because of the
    strongly fluidising behaviour that starch dextrinisation gives to the
    reconstituted porridges. This makes it possible, for the same viscosity
    threshold, to increase very appreciably the rate at which dry matter is
    incorporated into the reconstituted porridges — and therefore to increase
    their nutritional and energy density by as much.
  </p>
  <p>
    Another factor to take into account is how cooked and how instant these
    flours are. They fall into three groups:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>flours to be cooked, which still need at least 15&nbsp;minutes of cooking,</li>
    <li>precooked flours, to be boiled for 5 to 10&nbsp;minutes,</li>
    <li>instant flours, requiring no further cooking.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Starch digestibility is correlated with its degree of gelatinisation, a
    process known to develop through cooking in excess water. As regards
    extrusion cooking, this explains why a wet process is preferable to a dry
    one. It also makes it possible to precook the starch base first and then,
    after processing, to add the heat-sensitive vitamins as well as any
    protein enrichment (milk proteins) and sugar. Such a protocol prevents the
    development of Maillard reactions, which would, under the effect of
    cooking, immobilise the lysine by reaction with the sugar. Instant flours
    also limit the energy cost and preparation time: they improve convenience
    while optimising nutritional and hygienic quality (sterilising cooking),
    as well as the preservation of the flours.
  </p>

  <h2>Nutritional supplements used in food aid</h2>
  <p>
    In humanitarian contexts, precooked flours can be incorporated into the
    basic food rations distributed to general beneficiaries. For higher-risk
    groups — young children, pregnant or breastfeeding women, patients
    suffering from various conditions, including AIDS — they can be added as
    supplementary rations to strengthen nutritional intake, as prevention
    against malnutrition or as treatment of moderate malnutrition.
  </p>
  <p>
    Between 200,000 and 300,000&nbsp;tonnes of such flours are distributed
    each year by the World Food Programme, a large part of them produced
    locally and almost exclusively by extrusion cooking. Since this process
    allows a wide range of raw materials to be incorporated, each country can
    adapt its recipes to locally available resources.
  </p>
